/-!
# Generic finiteness: a finite-type map with finite fibre over a minimal prime

Let `R → S` be of finite type, let `𝔭` be a minimal prime of `R` and assume only finitely many
primes of `S` lie over `𝔭`. Writing `R_𝔭` and `S_𝔭` for the localizations at `𝔭` (the latter at
the image of `𝔭.primeCompl` in `S`), the fibre `S ⊗_R κ(𝔭)` is a finite `κ(𝔭)`-algebra, and it is
`S_𝔭/𝔭S_𝔭`. Since `𝔭` is minimal, `R_𝔭` has a single prime, so `𝔭R_𝔭` is its nilradical and
`𝔭S_𝔭` consists of nilpotents; a monic equation over `R_𝔭` satisfied modulo `𝔭S_𝔭` therefore
becomes an honest one after raising it to a power, so `S_𝔭` is integral over `R_𝔭`. Clearing
denominators in the finitely many algebra generators inverts a single `g ∉ 𝔭`, and `S_g` is then
generated over `R_g` by integral elements, hence finite.

## Main results

* `Stacks.QuasiFinite.GenericFiniteness.solution`: the statement of
  `Algebra.exists_localization_away_map_finite_of_mem_minimalPrimes_of_finite_primesOver`.

## Implementation notes

The mathematics is carried by
`Stacks.QuasiFinite.GenericFiniteness.isIntegral_localization_of_mem_minimalPrimes`: over a minimal
prime with finite fibre, `S_𝔭` is integral over `R_𝔭`. The three results the source cites enter
there. Stacks 0H8X is `Stacks.QuasiFinite.GenericFiniteness.finite_fiber_of_finite_primesOver`: the
primes of `S` over `𝔭` are the points of `Spec` of the fibre, by
`PrimeSpectrum.primesOverOrderIsoFiber`, and a finite-type algebra over a field with finite
spectrum is a finite module. Stacks 00EU is `Ring.KrullDimLE 0 R_𝔭`, whose nilradical is the
maximal ideal, so `𝔭R_𝔭` consists of nilpotents. Stacks 0544 is
`Stacks.QuasiFinite.GenericFiniteness.map_le_nilradical`: an ideal of nilpotents extends to an
ideal of nilpotents, since `Ideal.map` is generated by the images of its elements.

The descent of the coefficients is
`Stacks.QuasiFinite.GenericFiniteness.exists_isIntegral_smul_of_isIntegral_localization`, in two
steps rather than by manipulating denominators:
`IsIntegral.exists_multiple_integral_of_isLocalization` moves the base from `R_𝔭` back to `R` at
the cost of a factor from `𝔭.primeCompl`, and
`Stacks.QuasiFinite.GenericFiniteness.exists_isIntegral_smul_of_isIntegral_algebraMap` moves the
target from `S_𝔭` back to `S` at the cost of a second such factor, by scaling the roots of the
monic polynomial. The single `g` of the statement is the product of the finitely many factors so
obtained, one per algebra generator.

The passage from that integrality to the conclusion needs neither `𝔭` nor the finite fibre. Its
content is `Stacks.QuasiFinite.GenericFiniteness.adjoin_image_eq_top_of_isLocalizationAway`: a
generating set of `S` over `R` still generates `S_g` over `R_g`, because the denominators `1/gⁿ`
already come from the base. Since `g` is a unit in `R_g`, those generators are themselves integral
over `R_g`, so `Algebra.finite_adjoin_of_finite_of_isIntegral` finishes
`Stacks.QuasiFinite.GenericFiniteness.finite_awayMap_of_isIntegral_smul` for arbitrary models
`R_g`, `S_g`.

The fibre `Ideal.Fiber` is the base change `κ(𝔭) ⊗_R S`, whose `S`-algebra structure is the right
tensor factor; that instance is not global in Mathlib, so
`Algebra.TensorProduct.rightAlgebra` is enabled as a local instance for this file.

## References

* [StacksProject 02ML](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02ML)
* [StacksProject 0H8X](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0H8X)
* [StacksProject 00EU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00EU)
* [StacksProject 0544](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0544)
-/

namespace Stacks.QuasiFinite.GenericFiniteness

open Polynomial

attribute [local instance] Algebra.TensorProduct.rightAlgebra

/-- An element satisfying a monic polynomial up to nilpotents is integral. -/
private lemma isIntegral_of_isNilpotent_aeval {R A : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing A]
    [Algebra R A] {x : A} {P : R[X]} (hP : P.Monic) (h : IsNilpotent (aeval x P)) :
    IsIntegral R x := by
  obtain ⟨e, he⟩ := h
  exact ⟨P ^ e, hP.pow e, by rw [← Polynomial.aeval_def, map_pow, he]⟩

/-- An ideal of nilpotents extends to an ideal of nilpotents, since `Ideal.map` is generated by
the images of the elements of the ideal. This is Stacks 0544. -/
@[ega "III 5.4.6" "more general"]
private lemma map_le_nilradical {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B] (f : A →+* B)
    {I : Ideal A} (hI : I ≤ nilradical A) : I.map f ≤ nilradical B := by
  rw [Ideal.map_le_iff_le_comap]
  exact fun a ha => (mem_nilradical.mp (hI ha)).map f

/-- If `B ⧸ IB` is a finite `A`-module for an ideal `I` of `A` consisting of nilpotents, then `B`
is integral over `A`: a monic equation holding modulo `IB` becomes an honest one after raising it
to a power. -/
private lemma isIntegral_of_finite_quotient_of_le_nilradical {A B : Type*} [CommRing A]
    [CommRing B] [Algebra A B] {I : Ideal A} (hI : I ≤ nilradical A)
    [Module.Finite A (B ⧸ I.map (algebraMap A B))] : Algebra.IsIntegral A B := by
  refine ⟨fun b => ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨P, hP, hPe⟩ :=
    (Algebra.IsIntegral.of_finite A (B ⧸ I.map (algebraMap A B))).isIntegral
      (Ideal.Quotient.mk _ b)
  refine isIntegral_of_isNilpotent_aeval hP
    (mem_nilradical.mp (map_le_nilradical (algebraMap A B) hI ?_))
  have h2 : (Ideal.Quotient.mkₐ A (I.map (algebraMap A B))) (aeval b P) = 0 := by
    rw [← Polynomial.aeval_algHom_apply]; exact hPe
  exact Ideal.Quotient.eq_zero_iff_mem.mp h2

/-- The fibre of a finite-type algebra over a prime with finitely many primes above it is a
finite-dimensional algebra over the residue field. The primes of `S` over `p` are the points of
`Spec` of the fibre, so this is the implication (3) `⇒` (2) of Stacks 0H8X. -/
private lemma finite_fiber_of_finite_primesOver {R S : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing S]
    [Algebra R S] [Algebra.FiniteType R S] (p : Ideal R) [p.IsPrime]
    (hfin : (p.primesOver S).Finite) : Module.Finite p.ResidueField (p.Fiber S) := by
  have := hfin.to_subtype
  have : Finite (PrimeSpectrum (p.Fiber S)) :=
    Finite.of_equiv _ (PrimeSpectrum.primesOverOrderIsoFiber R S p).toEquiv
  have : Algebra.QuasiFinite p.ResidueField (p.Fiber S) :=
    Algebra.QuasiFinite.iff_finite_comap_preimage_singleton.mpr fun _ => Set.toFinite _
  exact Module.Finite.of_quasiFinite

/-- **Integrality over the local ring at a minimal prime.** If `R → S` is of finite type, `𝔭` is a
minimal prime of `R` and only finitely many primes of `S` lie over `𝔭`, then `S_𝔭` is integral
over `R_𝔭`: the quotient `S_𝔭 ⧸ 𝔭S_𝔭` is the fibre, hence a finite `R_𝔭`-module, and `𝔭R_𝔭` is
the nilradical of `R_𝔭` because `𝔭` is minimal. -/
private lemma isIntegral_localization_of_mem_minimalPrimes {R S : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing S]
    [Algebra R S] [Algebra.FiniteType R S] {𝔭 : Ideal R} [𝔭.IsPrime]
    (h𝔭 : 𝔭 ∈ minimalPrimes R) (hfin : (𝔭.primesOver S).Finite) :
    Algebra.IsIntegral (Localization 𝔭.primeCompl)
      (Localization (Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid S 𝔭.primeCompl)) := by
  -- The fibre over `𝔭` is a finite `κ(𝔭)`-algebra, hence a finite `R_𝔭`-module.
  have : Module.Finite 𝔭.ResidueField (𝔭.Fiber S) := finite_fiber_of_finite_primesOver 𝔭 hfin
  have : Module.Finite (Localization 𝔭.primeCompl) (𝔭.Fiber S) :=
    Module.Finite.trans 𝔭.ResidueField _
  -- and `S_𝔭 ⧸ 𝔭S_𝔭` is that fibre.
  have : Module.Finite (Localization 𝔭.primeCompl)
      (Localization (Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid S 𝔭.primeCompl) ⧸
        (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (Localization 𝔭.primeCompl)).map
          (algebraMap (Localization 𝔭.primeCompl)
            (Localization (Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid S 𝔭.primeCompl)))) :=
    Module.Finite.equiv (AlgEquiv.extendScalarsOfIsLocalization (Localization 𝔭.primeCompl)
      𝔭.primeCompl ((Ideal.Fiber.algEquivQuotient (S := S) 𝔭).restrictScalars R)).toLinearEquiv
  -- Minimality of `𝔭` makes `𝔭R_𝔭` the nilradical of `R_𝔭`.
  have : Ring.KrullDimLE 0 (Localization 𝔭.primeCompl) :=
    Ring.KrullDimLE.of_isLocalization 𝔭 h𝔭 _
  exact isIntegral_of_finite_quotient_of_le_nilradical
    (Ring.KrullDimLE.nilradical_eq_maximalIdeal (Localization 𝔭.primeCompl)).ge

/-- If the image of `y : S` in a localization `T` of `S` at the image of a submonoid `M` of `R` is
integral over `R`, then `w • y` is integral over `R` for some `w ∈ M`: a witness `w` killing the
monic equation of the image scales the roots of that equation. -/
private lemma exists_isIntegral_smul_of_isIntegral_algebraMap {R S T : Type*} [CommRing R]
    [CommRing S] [CommRing T] [Algebra R S] [Algebra S T] [Algebra R T] [IsScalarTower R S T]
    (M : Submonoid R) [IsLocalization (Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid S M) T] {y : S}
    (h : IsIntegral R (algebraMap S T y)) :
    ∃ w : M, IsIntegral R ((w : R) • y) := by
  simp only [Algebra.smul_def]
  obtain ⟨P, hP, hPe⟩ := h
  have h0 : algebraMap S T (aeval y P) = 0 := by
    rw [← Polynomial.aeval_algebraMap_apply]; exact hPe
  obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ :=
    (IsLocalization.map_eq_zero_iff (Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid S M) T _).mp h0
  obtain ⟨w, hwM, hwc⟩ := c.2
  rw [← hwc] at hc
  refine ⟨⟨w, hwM⟩, ?_⟩
  change IsIntegral R (algebraMap R S w * y)
  rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos P.natDegree with hd | hd
  · -- A constant monic equation forces `w = 0` in `S`.
    have hP1 : P = 1 := hP.natDegree_eq_zero.mp hd
    have hw0 : algebraMap R S w = 0 := by simpa [hP1] using hc
    rw [hw0, zero_mul]
    exact isIntegral_zero
  · refine ⟨P.scaleRoots w, (Polynomial.monic_scaleRoots_iff _).mpr hP, ?_⟩
    obtain ⟨d, hd'⟩ := Nat.exists_eq_succ_of_ne_zero hd.ne'
    rw [Polynomial.scaleRoots_eval₂_mul, hd', pow_succ, mul_assoc, ← Polynomial.aeval_def, hc,
      mul_zero]

/-- **Descent of integrality along a localization.** If the localization of `S` at the image of a
submonoid `M` of `R` is integral over the localization of `R` at `M`, then every `x : S` becomes
integral over `R` after multiplication by a suitable element of `M`: one factor of `M` clears the
denominators of the coefficients, a second one those of the root. -/
private lemma exists_isIntegral_smul_of_isIntegral_localization {R S Rₘ Sₘ : Type*} [CommRing R]
    [CommRing S] [CommRing Rₘ] [CommRing Sₘ] [Algebra R S] [Algebra R Rₘ] [Algebra S Sₘ]
    [Algebra Rₘ Sₘ] [Algebra R Sₘ] [IsScalarTower R S Sₘ] [IsScalarTower R Rₘ Sₘ]
    (M : Submonoid R) [IsLocalization M Rₘ]
    [IsLocalization (Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid S M) Sₘ] [Algebra.IsIntegral Rₘ Sₘ] (x : S) :
    ∃ w : M, IsIntegral R ((w : R) • x) := by
  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := IsIntegral.exists_multiple_integral_of_isLocalization (Rₘ := Rₘ) M
    (algebraMap S Sₘ x) (Algebra.IsIntegral.isIntegral (R := Rₘ) _)
  obtain ⟨w, hw⟩ := exists_isIntegral_smul_of_isIntegral_algebraMap (T := Sₘ) M
    (y := (m : R) • x) (by
      rw [Algebra.smul_def, map_mul, ← IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply, ← Algebra.smul_def]
      simpa [Submonoid.smul_def] using hm)
  exact ⟨w * m, by rw [Submonoid.coe_mul, mul_smul]; exact hw⟩

/-- Integrality is insensitive to a unit factor from the base ring. -/
private lemma isIntegral_of_smul_of_isUnit {R A : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing A] [Algebra R A]
    {r : R} (hr : IsUnit r) {x : A} (h : IsIntegral R (r • x)) : IsIntegral R x := by
  obtain ⟨v, hv⟩ := hr.exists_left_inv
  simpa [smul_smul, hv] using h.smul v

/-- **Algebra generators survive a localization away from an element of the base.** If `t`
generates `S` as an `R`-algebra and `g : R`, then the image of `t` generates `S_g` as an
`R_g`-algebra: an element of `S_g` is `y/gⁿ` with `y : S`, and the denominator `1/gⁿ` already
comes from `R_g`. -/
private lemma adjoin_image_eq_top_of_isLocalizationAway {R S Rg Sg : Type*} [CommRing R]
    [CommRing S] [CommRing Rg] [CommRing Sg] [Algebra R S] [Algebra R Rg] [Algebra S Sg]
    [Algebra Rg Sg] [Algebra R Sg] [IsScalarTower R S Sg] [IsScalarTower R Rg Sg]
    {t : Set S} (ht : Algebra.adjoin R t = ⊤) (g : R) [IsLocalization.Away g Rg]
    [IsLocalization.Away (algebraMap R S g) Sg] :
    Algebra.adjoin Rg (algebraMap S Sg '' t) = ⊤ := by
  set T := Algebra.adjoin Rg (algebraMap S Sg '' t)
  have himage (y : S) : algebraMap S Sg y ∈ T := by
    have h1 : Algebra.adjoin R t ≤ (T.restrictScalars R).comap (IsScalarTower.toAlgHom R S Sg) :=
      Algebra.adjoin_le fun x hx => Algebra.subset_adjoin ⟨x, hx, rfl⟩
    rw [ht] at h1
    exact h1 Algebra.mem_top
  refine top_unique fun z _ => ?_
  obtain ⟨n, y, hy⟩ := IsLocalization.Away.surj (algebraMap R S g) z
  have hg : algebraMap S Sg (algebraMap R S g) = algebraMap Rg Sg (algebraMap R Rg g) := by
    rw [← IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply, ← IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply]
  have hAB : (algebraMap Rg Sg (IsLocalization.Away.invSelf g)) ^ n *
      (algebraMap Rg Sg (algebraMap R Rg g)) ^ n = 1 := by
    rw [← mul_pow, mul_comm, ← map_mul, IsLocalization.Away.mul_invSelf, map_one, one_pow]
  have hz : z = algebraMap Rg Sg (IsLocalization.Away.invSelf g ^ n) * algebraMap S Sg y := by
    rw [← hy, hg, map_pow, ← mul_assoc, mul_comm _ z, mul_assoc, hAB, mul_one]
  rw [hz]
  exact T.mul_mem (T.algebraMap_mem _) (himage y)

/-- **Finiteness of an away-localization from integrality after clearing one denominator.**
If `S` is generated as an `R`-algebra by a finite set `t`, and `g • x` is integral over `R` for
every `x ∈ t`, then the canonical map `R_g → S_g` on localizations away from `g` and from its
image is finite, for arbitrary models `R_g`, `S_g`. Indeed `g` becomes invertible in `R_g`, so
each generator is itself integral over `R_g`, and it still generates. -/
private lemma finite_awayMap_of_isIntegral_smul {R S : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing S]
    [Algebra R S] {t : Set S} (ht : Algebra.adjoin R t = ⊤) (htfin : t.Finite) (g : R)
    (hgt : ∀ x ∈ t, IsIntegral R (g • x)) (Rg Sg : Type*) [CommRing Rg] [CommRing Sg]
    [Algebra R Rg] [Algebra S Sg] [IsLocalization.Away g Rg]
    [IsLocalization.Away (algebraMap R S g) Sg] :
    (IsLocalization.Away.map Rg Sg (algebraMap R S) g).Finite := by
  let _ : Algebra R Sg := ((algebraMap S Sg).comp (algebraMap R S)).toAlgebra
  have : IsScalarTower R S Sg := IsScalarTower.of_algebraMap_eq' rfl
  let _ : Algebra Rg Sg := (IsLocalization.Away.map Rg Sg (algebraMap R S) g).toAlgebra
  have : IsScalarTower R Rg Sg := IsScalarTower.of_algebraMap_eq' (by
    change (algebraMap S Sg).comp (algebraMap R S) =
      (IsLocalization.Away.map Rg Sg (algebraMap R S) g).comp (algebraMap R Rg)
    exact (IsLocalization.map_comp _).symm)
  have hint : ∀ z ∈ algebraMap S Sg '' t, IsIntegral Rg z := by
    rintro _ ⟨x, hx, rfl⟩
    refine isIntegral_of_smul_of_isUnit (IsLocalization.Away.algebraMap_isUnit (S := Rg) g) ?_
    rw [IsScalarTower.algebraMap_smul Rg]
    simpa using ((hgt x hx).map (IsScalarTower.toAlgHom R S Sg)).tower_top (A := Rg)
  have hfin : Module.Finite Rg (Algebra.adjoin Rg (algebraMap S Sg '' t)) :=
    Algebra.finite_adjoin_of_finite_of_isIntegral (htfin.image _) hint
  rw [adjoin_image_eq_top_of_isLocalizationAway ht g] at hfin
  exact Module.Finite.equiv Subalgebra.topEquiv.toLinearEquiv

/-- **Generic finiteness over a minimal prime.** If `R → S` is of finite type, `𝔭` is a minimal
prime of `R` and only finitely many primes of `S` lie over `𝔭`, then some `g ∉ 𝔭` makes
`R_g → S_g` finite, for all models `R_g`, `S_g` of the localizations away from `g` and its image.
-/
public theorem solution {R S : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]
    [Algebra.FiniteType R S] {𝔭 : Ideal R}
    (h𝔭 : 𝔭 ∈ minimalPrimes R) (hfin : (𝔭.primesOver S).Finite) :
    ∃ g ∉ 𝔭, ∀ (Rg Sg : Type*) [CommRing Rg] [CommRing Sg] [Algebra R Rg] [Algebra S Sg]
      [IsLocalization.Away g Rg] [IsLocalization.Away (algebraMap R S g) Sg],
      (IsLocalization.Away.map Rg Sg (algebraMap R S) g).Finite := by
  classical
  have : 𝔭.IsPrime := h𝔭.1.1
  have : Algebra.IsIntegral (Localization 𝔭.primeCompl)
      (Localization (Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid S 𝔭.primeCompl)) :=
    isIntegral_localization_of_mem_minimalPrimes h𝔭 hfin
  choose w hw using fun x : S => exists_isIntegral_smul_of_isIntegral_localization
    (Rₘ := Localization 𝔭.primeCompl)
    (Sₘ := Localization (Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid S 𝔭.primeCompl)) 𝔭.primeCompl x
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := ‹Algebra.FiniteType R S›.out
  -- One denominator per generator; their product is the `g` of the statement.
  refine ⟨∏ x ∈ s, (w x : R), Submonoid.prod_mem _ fun x _ => (w x).2,
    fun Rg Sg _ _ _ _ _ _ =>
      finite_awayMap_of_isIntegral_smul hs s.finite_toSet _ (fun x hx => ?_) Rg Sg⟩
  rw [← Finset.prod_erase_mul s _ hx, mul_smul]
  exact (hw x).smul _

end Stacks.QuasiFinite.GenericFiniteness

/-!
# Generic finiteness: a finite-type map with finite fibre over a minimal prime

Let `R → S` be a ring map of finite type, let `𝔭` be a minimal prime of `R`, and assume that only
finitely many primes of `S` lie over `𝔭`. Then there is a `g ∈ R`, `g ∉ 𝔭`, such that
`R_g → S_g` is finite.

The mechanism is that minimality of `𝔭` makes `𝔭R_𝔭` locally nilpotent, so that the finitely many
algebra generators `x_i` of `S`, which satisfy monic equations over `R_𝔭` modulo `𝔭S_𝔭` because
`S ⊗_R κ(𝔭)` is a finite `κ(𝔭)`-algebra, satisfy honest monic equations over `R_𝔭` after raising
`P_i(x_i)` to a power; clearing denominators in the finitely many coefficients and in the finitely
many relations involves inverting a single element `g ∉ 𝔭`. Geometrically: a morphism of finite
type whose fibre over the generic point of an irreducible component of the base is finite is
already finite over a standard open neighbourhood of that generic point.

## Main results

* `Algebra.exists_localization_away_map_finite_of_mem_minimalPrimes_of_finite_primesOver`: the
  lemma above, for an arbitrary pair of models of the two localizations away from `g`.

## Implementation notes

"There are at most finitely many primes of `S` lying over `𝔭`" is `(𝔭.primesOver S).Finite`,
`Ideal.primesOver` being `{𝔮 | 𝔮.IsPrime ∧ 𝔮.LiesOver 𝔭}`, that is, the set of primes of `S`
contracting to `𝔭` along `algebraMap R S` — literally the fibre of `Spec S → Spec R` over `𝔭`.
"`𝔭` is a minimal prime" is `𝔭 ∈ minimalPrimes R`, the minimal primes of `⊥`; it supplies
`𝔭.IsPrime` (as `h𝔭.1.1`), so no separate primality hypothesis is carried.

The two localizations are abstract: `Rg` is any `R`-algebra with `IsLocalization.Away g`, and `Sg`
any `S`-algebra with `IsLocalization.Away (algebraMap R S g)`, so that the conclusion applies to
whichever models a user has, and the source's `R_g → S_g` is the canonical lift
`IsLocalization.Away.map` of `algebraMap R S`, which exists for every such pair with no further
data. The source's concrete reading is the case `Rg = Localization.Away g`,
`Sg = Localization.Away (algebraMap R S g)`, where `IsLocalization.Away.map` is
`Localization.awayMap` by definition.

Finiteness of that map is `RingHom.Finite`, as in its sibling
`RingHom.localization_away_map_finite`. This is the form that needs neither an `Algebra Rg Sg`
instance nor scalar towers as hypotheses — no such instance exists for the concrete
localizations — and a user who does have them converts to `Module.Finite Rg Sg` by
`RingHom.finite_algebraMap`, the two maps agreeing by `IsLocalization.ringHom_ext` since both
restrict to `R → Sg`.

No Noetherian or domain hypothesis is imposed; the source's proof uses none.

Faithfulness. (1) No hypothesis is dead. `Algebra.FiniteType R S` is live: for `R = k` a field and
`S = k(x)` the prime `⊥` is minimal and is the only prime of `S`, yet `R_g = k` for every `g ≠ 0`
and `k(x)` is not a finite `k`-module. Minimality of `𝔭` is live: for `R = k[t]`,
`S = R[x]/(tx - 1) = k[t, 1/t]` and `𝔭 = (t)`, no prime of `S` lies over `𝔭`, but for `g ∉ (t)` the
map `k[t, 1/g] → k[t, 1/(tg)]` is not finite (its Spec would then have closed image, which misses
only the point `(t)`). Finiteness of `𝔭.primesOver S` is live: for `R = ℚ[t]`, `S = ℚ[t, y]` and
`𝔭 = ⊥`, the primes `(y - c)` all lie over `⊥` and no localization `R_g → S_g` is finite.
(2) The scalars and the base are the source's: finiteness is of `Sg` as a module over `Rg`, the
localization of `R` away from `g` — not over `R` and not over `S` — the finite type hypothesis is
over `R`, and `primesOver` contracts along `algebraMap R S`. (3) The canonical map is pinned, not
merely asserted to exist: `Rg` and `Sg` are pinned as localizations away from `g` and from its
image `algebraMap R S g`, and the map whose finiteness is claimed is the canonical
`IsLocalization.Away.map Rg Sg (algebraMap R S) g`, not an unspecified ring map `Rg → Sg`.
(4) The instantiation is not degenerate: for `R = ℤ`, `S = ℤ[x]/(2x - 1) = ℤ[1/2]` and `𝔭 = ⊥`,
the hypotheses hold (`S` is a domain, so `⊥` is its only prime over `⊥`) and `g = 2` works, while
`g` a unit does not, `ℤ[1/2]` not being a finite `ℤ`-module; for `S = ℤ[x]/(2x² - 1)` the localized
map is finite of rank `2` rather than an isomorphism.

